
#   Hibernate Cache

##  First level Cache
    This first level cache is the initial cache Hibernate retrieving an object from the DB.
    This cache is maintainer at the Session level and enabled by default in Hibernate.
    First Level cache is associated with the session object.
    The data stored in the first level cache is accessible to the only session that maintains it.
    You cannot disable the first level cache, it is always enabled.
    Hibernate entities remain in cache only until session is open, once session is closed all associated
    cache data is lost
    Mainly, it reduced the number of SQL queries generated within a given transaction. Instead of updating after
    every modification done in the transaction, it updates the transaction only at the end of transaction/session

##  First level cache causes Reduction in SQL queries
    An entity loaded within a session is stored the first level cache.
    If the same entity is requested again during that session, Hibernated retrieves it from the cache.
    This avoids executing additional SQL queries against the DB
    Hence, the number of SQL queries is reduced.
    Multiple accesses to the same entity within a session do not result in repeated db interactions.

## First -level cache causes delayed Execution of SQL Updates
   Hibernate tracks changes to entities within a session
   Modification to entities are recorded by session but not immediately applied to db. Hibernate waits until
   transaction is ready to commit
   At transaction end, Hibernate flushes the session, which means changes are synced to the DB.
   This batching of SQL updates enhances efficiency by minimizing DB interactions.

## Remove/ clear cache
   When we query an entity first time, it is retrieved from DB and stored in first level cache
   Session.evict() can be used to remove the loaded entity from first level cache.
   The next loading of this entity will again make a db call if it has been removed using evict() method.
   The whole session cache can be removed using clear() method. It will remove all the entities stored in cache
   Multiple accesses to the same entity within a session do not result in repeated database interactions.

   Conclusion:-
   - First level cache in Hibernated reduces SQL queries
   - Caches entities within a session defers SQL updates until end of transaction
   - Ensures efficient and optimized database operations
   - As soon as you close the session, all data in first level cache is lost
   - If you load same object again, Hibernate will again go the the DB to load that object, provided Second level
     cache is disabled.
